WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.261
Picture a night in
a small town in Tamil Nadu.

2
00:00:03.261 --> 00:00:08.451
A woman is in pain, and her daughter turns
to the local health worker with a

3
00:00:08.451 --> 00:00:09.680
question in Tamil.

4
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.086
Her pain is worse
at night. What should we do?

5
00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.833
Palli Sahayak is a voice assistant
for palliative care, and it's open source.

6
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:25.386
You ask out loud, in your own
language, by an ordinary phone call,

7
00:00:25.386 --> 00:00:28.284
WhatsApp, or the web. No new app to learn.

8
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.177
Why look things up first?

9
00:00:31.177 --> 00:00:36.281
A model answering from memory can
sound sure and still be wrong. In medicine,

10
00:00:36.281 --> 00:00:37.408
that's dangerous.

11
00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:42.662
It searches trusted care guidelines for the
passages closest in meaning to the

12
00:00:42.662 --> 00:00:44.587
question, and keeps only those.

13
00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:50.685
Then a language model turns them into
a short, warm reply, in the caller's language.

14
00:00:50.685 --> 00:00:55.322
The facts come from the guidelines; the
model makes them easy to hear.

15
00:00:56.353 --> 00:01:00.774
Here's a short excerpt from a completely
fictional conversation: made up,

16
00:01:00.774 --> 00:01:01.985
with no real people.

17
00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:06.897
A daughter, in Tamil.

18
00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:09.660
Caregiver (daughter): Yes, they have.

19
00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:14.685
But… we only give it when the pain comes, the
lady next door said she'd get used to

20
00:01:14.685 --> 00:01:14.866
it.

21
00:01:16.053 --> 00:01:20.898
Palli Sahayak: Give it just
as the doctor said. If times are set,

22
00:01:20.898 --> 00:01:25.221
don't wait for the pain.
Don't change the amount yourself;

23
00:01:25.221 --> 00:01:27.680
if you're unsure, ask the doctor.

24
00:01:28.653 --> 00:01:32.123
Palli Sahayak: Talk
to your nurse or doctor today.

25
00:01:32.123 --> 00:01:34.690
If the pain suddenly gets much worse,

26
00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:40.380
call 108 for emergency help right away, and
tell your nurse too. You're not alone.

27
00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:47.377
Notice the guard rails. It
never gives specific medicine doses.

28
00:01:47.377 --> 00:01:52.655
If it hears danger signs, it tells
the caller to call one zero eight right away,

29
00:01:52.655 --> 00:01:56.218
and flags the call as
urgent for clinicians to review.

30
00:01:57.553 --> 00:02:00.740
We're now running a much
more rigorous and detailed study,

31
00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:05.299
with oncology and palliative care clinical
partners across India, to evaluate Palli

32
00:02:05.299 --> 00:02:08.760
Sahayak and generate evidence on its
benefit and effectiveness.

33
00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:13.320
And given the positive, encouraging
response to phase one's palliative care advice,

34
00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.287
we're also considering extending
it to women's health,

35
00:02:16.287 --> 00:02:19.308
starting with personalised care
advice after menopause.

36
00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.230
Palli Sahayak is modular by design.

37
00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:25.463
A new specialty mainly needs
its care guidelines.

38
00:02:25.463 --> 00:02:30.082
A new language mainly needs speech-to-text
and text-to-speech support,

39
00:02:30.082 --> 00:02:33.910
from a speech model. And
a new country works the same way:

40
00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:38.991
for Kenya or Zambia, add their palliative
care guidelines to the library, and

41
00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:40.904
change the language settings.

42
00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:47.296
Palli Sahayak is an ongoing project, and it
has not yet been tested with real

43
00:02:47.296 --> 00:02:47.878
patients.

44
00:02:48.487 --> 00:02:51.149
The code is open
for anyone to read and use.

45
00:02:51.149 --> 00:02:56.170
And we're very grateful to the Gates
Foundation and BIRAC for supporting us through

46
00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.408
the Grand Challenges India programme.
